        </p>

        <div id="is-it-mold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 1. Is It Mold? Rule Out Efflorescence First
          </h2>

          <p>
            White fuzz on a basement wall is not always mold, and the difference decides whether you are facing an afternoon with a stiff brush or a weekend of cutting out drywall. Efflorescence is salt. Water carries dissolved minerals through concrete or block, evaporates at the surface, and leaves a white or grey crystalline deposit behind. Mold is a living organism, and it needs something organic to eat: wood joists, the paper facing on drywall, cardboard boxes, carpet backing, even the film of dust and dirt that settles on bare concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three quick checks settle most cases. Rub it with a gloved finger or a dry brush: efflorescence crumbles into dry powder or small crystals, while mold smears, looks fuzzy or slimy, and often has a raised texture. Mist it with water: salt deposits dissolve and disappear, mold does not. Put a few drops of white vinegar on it: most efflorescence is carbonate-based and fizzes. Colour helps too. Efflorescence is almost always white, while mold can be white but is just as often green, grey, black or blue-green, and frequently grows in rounded, spotty colonies rather than lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Location is the other giveaway. Efflorescence shows up on bare masonry, often tracing mortar joints, the lower few feet of the wall, or the edges of a crack where water is coming through. Mold favours the sill plate and rim joist where the house framing sits on the foundation, the bottoms of drywall and paneling, stored belongings, the underside of stair treads, and anywhere air sits still. Smell is the tiebreaker: mold gives off the earthy, musty odour everyone recognises, and salt has no smell at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can easily have both. The same moisture that deposits salt on the block also feeds mold on the framing a foot away, so a white crust on the wall is a warning that the space is wet enough to grow mold elsewhere. If the deposit turns out to be salt, our guide to <a href="/efflorescence-on-basement-walls/">efflorescence on basement walls</a> covers removing it and what it says about the wall. If it is mold, keep reading. And if the basement smells musty but you cannot see anything, look behind and under things: the back of drywall, under carpet and pad, inside boxes, around the sump pit and on the furnace or air handler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not spend money on a mold test to find out what species it is. The EPA says that in most cases, if visible mold growth is present, sampling is unnecessary, and there are no federal limits for what counts as an acceptable spore level indoors. Colour does not tell you how hazardous a mold is, and every type gets removed the same way. Do-it-yourself test kits almost always grow something because spores are present in every home. The exceptions where a professional assessment earns its fee are a strong musty smell with no visible source, a house you are about to buy, or someone in the house with health problems their doctor links to the space.
          </p>

