        <div id="gear-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 4. Protective Gear and Setting Up the Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            The EPA's minimum kit for a small cleanup is an N-95 respirator, gloves that extend to the middle of the forearm, and goggles without ventilation holes. Use a NIOSH-approved N95 that fits snugly; a loose paper dust mask does almost nothing, and a beard breaks the seal on any disposable respirator. Pick nitrile, neoprene or rubber gloves rather than latex if you will be using any cleaner beyond plain detergent. Wear old clothes you can wash hot straight afterwards, or a pair of disposable coveralls, and leave them in the basement rather than walking through the house in them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn off the furnace or air conditioner before you disturb anything, and tape plastic over any supply or return vents in the basement. A forced-air system that is running while you scrub will pull loose spores into the ductwork and spread them upstairs. Leave it off until you have finished cleaning and vacuuming.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then contain the work. Close the basement door and hang plastic sheeting over the stair opening, taped on all sides. If there is a basement window, put a box fan in it blowing out. That creates a slight negative pressure, so air leaks into the work area from the rest of the house instead of the other way round, and it carries airborne spores outside. Move belongings you want to keep out of the area or bag them, and lay plastic on the floor under any drywall you plan to cut out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the dust down as you work. Lightly mist moldy material with water or detergent solution before you scrub, cut or bag it; dry brushing and dry scraping throw spores into the air. Do not use an ordinary shop vac on mold, because its exhaust blows the finest particles straight back out. A vacuum with a sealed true HEPA filter is the right tool for the final clean-up, and many tool-rental counters stock them if you do not own one.
          </p>

          <p>
            An air purifier earns a place here as a supporting tool. A true HEPA unit running in the work area and another in the room above the stairs will capture spores knocked loose during cleanup and keep levels down while things dry. It will not remove a single colony from a wall, though, so think of it as cleanup support rather than the cure. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">best air purifiers for mold</a> guide covers which units have the airflow for a basement-sized space.
          </p>

