        <div id="step-by-step" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 5. How to Get Rid of Mold in a Basement, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the procedure for a job inside the EPA's do-it-yourself range, once you have stopped the water or at least know how you are going to. It follows the EPA's home guidance: remove what cannot be cleaned, scrub what can, and dry everything fast.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Remove porous materials that are moldy</h3>

          <p>
            Drywall, ceiling tiles, carpet and pad, fiberglass insulation, cardboard and upholstered items that have grown mold are not worth cleaning. The EPA notes that absorbent materials such as ceiling tiles and carpet may have to be thrown away once moldy, because growth runs into the material where scrubbing cannot reach. Mist them, cut drywall out well past the visible edge of the growth, check the back of each piece and the cavity behind it, and seal everything in heavy plastic bags before carrying it out, ideally through a window rather than the house. In most areas bagged mold debris can go out with ordinary household trash, but check your local rules for large loads.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Scrub hard surfaces with detergent and water</h3>

          <p>
            Concrete, block, metal, glass, tile and hard plastic get the EPA's basic treatment: scrub the mold off with detergent and water, then dry completely. A squirt of ordinary dish or laundry detergent in a bucket of warm water and a stiff nylon brush will do it. Work from the top down and from clean areas towards the growth, change the water when it gets cloudy, and wipe the surface down with clean water and a rag afterwards so you do not leave a film of loosened spores and soap behind. Bare concrete is porous, so give it a firm scrub and expect to repeat the treatment on heavy patches.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Clean wood framing</h3>

          <p>
            Joists, studs, the sill plate and the rim joist sit between the two categories. Scrub them with the same detergent solution. If growth remains in the grain after it dries, sanding or wire-brushing the surface removes it, with the respirator on and a HEPA vacuum close by. Probe the wood with a screwdriver: if it is soft or crumbles, it is rotted and needs replacing, which is a carpentry job. Dark staining sometimes remains on clean, dry wood. Professionals often seal cleaned framing with a mold-resistant encapsulant at this stage, but only once it is clean and fully dry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Dry everything within 24 to 48 hours</h3>

          <p>
            The EPA's window for drying water-damaged areas is 24 to 48 hours, and the same urgency applies after a wet cleanup. Run a dehumidifier flat out, set fans to move air across the cleaned surfaces, and keep the space closed off from humid outdoor air. A cheap pin-type moisture meter takes the guesswork out: most contractors want framing down to roughly 15 percent moisture content or lower before anything is closed back in, and concrete should read dry to the touch and stay that way after you lay a square of plastic on it overnight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: HEPA vacuum and wipe down</h3>

          <p>
            Once everything is dry, go over the whole basement with the HEPA vacuum, including floors, shelves, the tops of ducts and pipes, and the sill plate, then damp-wipe hard surfaces. This collects spores and debris that settled during the work. Bag the vacuum contents and the rags, take down the containment plastic, and only then restart the HVAC system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 6: Inspect, and do not paint over it</h3>

          <p>
            Look again after a week and after the next heavy rain. A returning musty smell or fresh spots in the same place means the moisture has not been solved. Do not reach for paint or caulk to cover what is left; the EPA is explicit that moldy surfaces should not be painted or caulked, and that you should clean and dry them first. Mold-resistant primer is a reasonable final coat on clean, dry concrete or drywall, not a substitute for the steps above.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. 6. Bleach, Vinegar and What Actually Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bleach is the first thing most people grab, and it is not what the EPA recommends. Its guidance says the use of a chemical or biocide that kills organisms such as mold, chlorine bleach for example, is not recommended as a routine practice during mold cleanup, though professional judgment may call for it in special cases such as homes with immune-compromised occupants. The reasoning is straightforward: the goal is to remove the mold, not just kill it. Dead mold and spores can still trigger allergies, and a background level of spores will always remain, which will not grow once the moisture problem is fixed.
          </p>

          <p>
            On porous and semi-porous basement materials, bleach also gives a false result. It whitens the stain so the wall looks clean, while the material is still damp and the growth inside it is still there. Household bleach is mostly water, so you are adding moisture to the exact surface you are trying to dry. In a closed basement the fumes are unpleasant at best. If you do use it on a hard, non-porous surface, ventilate the space, dilute it exactly as the label says, and follow the EPA's warning never to mix chlorine bleach with ammonia or cleaners containing ammonia, because the combination produces toxic fumes.
          </p>

          <p>
            White vinegar, hydrogen peroxide, borax and baking soda all have loyal followings. On hard surfaces they can help, but none of them is doing anything that detergent, a stiff brush and thorough drying will not. If you use them, use them one at a time. Vinegar mixed with bleach releases chlorine gas, and vinegar mixed with hydrogen peroxide in the same container forms peracetic acid, which is corrosive. Rinse the surface with water between products if you switch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Commercial mold removers are fine on hard surfaces as long as you follow the label. Any product that claims to kill mold has to carry an EPA registration number, and the label tells you dilution, contact time and the protective gear required. Those directions are not suggestions. But a product that kills mold does not lift it off the wall, so you still scrub and still remove porous material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip ozone generators and whole-room foggers. The EPA has stated that at concentrations that do not exceed public health standards, ozone has little potential to remove indoor air contaminants, and higher concentrations irritate the lungs. A fogger mists chemical over surfaces without removing any of the growth on them. Both are sold as shortcuts, and both leave the mold and the moisture exactly where they were.
          </p>

